From: MT6990 repair <noreply@example.invalid>
Subject: [PATCH] sched: dump owner of a corrupted stack end

The schedule-time stack end check currently panics without identifying the
task whose stack marker was corrupted.  That loses the most useful evidence
when the corruption is detected long after it happened.

Log the task identity, CPU, stack bounds and observed marker, then dump the
task stack before panicking.  Keep the existing fail-stop behaviour.

---
 kernel/sched/core.c | 10 +++++++++-
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/kernel/sched/core.c
+++ b/kernel/sched/core.c
@@ -5896,7 +5896,15 @@ static inline void schedule_debug(struct
 {
 #ifdef CONFIG_SCHED_STACK_END_CHECK
-	if (task_stack_end_corrupted(prev))
+	if (task_stack_end_corrupted(prev)) {
+		unsigned long *stack_end = end_of_stack(prev);
+
+		pr_emerg("corrupted stack end: task=%s pid=%d tgid=%d cpu=%d stack=%px end=%px value=%#lx expected=%#lx\n",
+			 prev->comm, prev->pid, prev->tgid, task_cpu(prev),
+			 task_stack_page(prev), stack_end, READ_ONCE(*stack_end),
+			 (unsigned long)STACK_END_MAGIC);
+		show_stack(prev, NULL, KERN_EMERG);
 		panic("corrupted stack end detected inside scheduler\n");
+	}
 
 	if (task_scs_end_corrupted(prev))
 		panic("corrupted shadow stack detected inside scheduler\n");
